 This is a qualitative contrast, not a controlled ablation of human involvement or drafting rules. Tasks, models, and revision histories differ, and later reviewed versions must not be treated as untouched initial outputs. The contrast does not redefine the main papers as autonomous experiments or suggest that every minimally supervised draft fails. It motivates preserving the stated experiment conditions and applying human-guided structure and narrow redline edits when the objective becomes a dependable paper for scientific readers.
 
+\section{Related approaches and current proposals}\label{app:related}
+This selective survey includes papers, teaching material, and explicitly identified first-party reports, with the research cutoff recorded in the companion repository. The aiXiv bibliographies supplied discovery leads; claims below were checked against the original sources rather than treating those drafts as independent corroboration. The companion repository retains source versions, inspected passages, and limitations.
+
+\textbf{Reproducibility and agent provenance.} CODECHECK organizes independent execution during review and explicitly separates checking execution from judging the scientific method~\cite{CODECHECK}. PROV-AGENT extends workflow provenance to connect agent prompts, responses, and decisions with downstream computations~\cite{PROVAGENT}. Such records make a result inspectable; they do not make an incorrect calculation correct. Hans and Bilionis's \emph{Paper-replication} implements a related coding-agent skill: selected claims become targets linked to executed evidence, comparisons, and report coverage, with a completion gate~\cite{PaperReplication}. Passing that gate concerns the recorded target set; their repeated runs still differ in numerical fidelity and acceptance rules. These approaches complement our emphasis on generated numerical macros, figure recipes, and adversarial review.
+
+\textbf{Autonomous research in computer science.} AI Scientist automates idea generation, experiments, and manuscript preparation within a supplied research environment; subsequent work develops the end-to-end AI-research program~\cite{AIScientist,AIResearch2026}. Agent Laboratory instead explicitly explores research assistance and human feedback~\cite{AgentLab}. AlphaEvolve evolves executable programs using human-supplied automatic evaluators~\cite{AlphaEvolve}. These are substantive demonstrations, with different scopes of autonomy. A machine-evaluable objective provides a particularly effective feedback loop, but the choice and scientific adequacy of that objective remain consequential. Our guide addresses how scientists make the resulting work verifiable and interpretable; it does not claim to demonstrate autonomous discovery.
+
+\textbf{Domain science and human validation.} Agentic systems also support domain science with differing human roles. Mephisto applies an agent to physical-component selection in galaxy spectral-energy-distribution modeling, using a domain code, structured diagnostics, and accumulated experience~\cite{Mephisto}. Co-Scientist generates and debates biological hypotheses, with experts selecting candidates and conducting experimental validation~\cite{CoScientist}. Kosmos combines literature search and data analysis, with traceable reports and sampled expert checks that distinguish analysis, literature, and synthesis claims~\cite{Kosmos}. Those distinctions matter: a retrieved citation, a reproduced calculation, and a plausible interpretation require different checks. Ting's hands-on astronomy course provides a complementary teaching route through tool execution, retrieval, agent memory, and safety~\cite{TingCourse}; it is instructional material rather than a performance study.
+
+\textbf{What an evaluation measures.} PaperBench evaluates replication of machine-learning research using graded implementation and execution evidence~\cite{PaperBench}. ReplicationBench uses expert-designed astrophysics tasks to assess both methodological faithfulness and correctness~\cite{ReplicationBench}. These test a broader task than finding a defect in an existing workflow. Their results depend on the dated model, harness, resources, and scoring rule; they are not measurements of the current frontier-model review capability described in this guide. Conversely, successful error diagnosis does not establish that an agent can independently choose and complete an important research program. ARA addresses another task: assessing reconstructability from the manuscript, without executing its code~\cite{ARA}. Document assessment and demonstrated reruns must remain distinguishable.
+
+\textbf{Fast-moving practice and proposals.} First-party engineering reports can document useful procedures before journal publication. Anthropic describes bounded delegation, evidence handling, and evaluation in a multi-agent web-research system~\cite{AnthropicResearch}; these observations concern its own system. OpenAI, PerturbAI, and Scripps report a recent large-scale analysis of biological data yielding candidate leads~\cite{OpenAIBiology}. Such leads should be distinguished from experimentally validated mechanisms. At the architectural level, Traxia proposes verifiable agent-native publishing, but explicitly presents a specification and partial prototype without empirical validation~\cite{Traxia}. These sources identify active directions rather than establish that a proposed safeguard works. The recurring practical question for this guide is what evidence another scientist can inspect and challenge.
+
